[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known, with one fix queued. Its reports describe different
problems. The queued parser fix closes `parser` only; `hint` keeps coming in after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket, and the parser fix covers only the <code>parser</code> row.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Each report is counted once (the export repeats some), in exactly one row. The rows are worked out
from the tenant, not from intake's tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the browser
is not where the date was typed.

### `parser`: day-first dates read month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads month first, so day and month
swap whenever the day is 12 or less: 7 April goes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names, and
the fix that is queued.

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form asks for `MM/DD/YYYY`

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type what the hint
says, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The date is swapped before any parser sees it, so the
parser fix does not help. Fix the hint (or use a date picker). These reports came in through the
form at booking-v3 tenants.

### Reports that read as a one-day move

A few reports describe a date one day off rather than swapped (the desk's notes list them). They
are not a separate problem: their tenants write the day first and they did not come through the
booking-v3 form, so their cause is `parser` and they are counted there, with the footnote under the
table.

## Why they were merged

Triage merges on the subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Bugs that all feel like "wrong date" share subject lines, so they merged into one ticket.
Read the body and the tenant before merging into a ticket that has a fix queued. Changing the rule
needs a new decision record, not an edit to 0001.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can be several bugs that sound the same. Count by cause, not by subject.
- Check what a fix covers before marking a ticket known.

